afk: the `auto` thinking level tops out at high instead of xhigh.

What must survive a rebase:
- One cap, at the classifier's ceiling. Upstream derives the ceiling from
  providers.autoThinkingMaxEffort, which only chooses xhigh vs max — so the
  distro cap is a fixed Effort.High ceiling (AUTO_EFFORT_CEILING since
  18.6.0; autoEffortCeiling() before) for both the online and the
  local-classifier branch, and the max question is never offered (since
  18.12.6 the online ladder is also filtered to tiers at or below the
  ceiling, so xhigh is not offered either).
- Explicit selections are untouched: a pinned level, and the `ultrathink`
  keyword (clamped at its own call site), still reach the model's max.

Rebased 18.1.13 -> 18.2.10: upstream rewrote the classifier around
resolveJudge/ChoiceQuestion (LEVEL_QUESTION vs LEVEL_QUESTION_WITH_MAX).
The cap still lives in autoEffortCeiling() (= Effort.High, so the max
question is never offered) plus the local-bucket branch's ceiling; the
clamp does the rest. Tests: `max` answered against a question that never
offered it now REJECTS (upstream semantics), so the opted-in cases assert
rejection instead of a snap-down; the local `hard` bucket row expects
high; one new xhigh-caps-at-high case.

Rebased 18.2.10 -> 18.6.0: upstream added solution-space questions
(REQUEST_QUESTIONS / SOLUTION_SPACE_QUESTIONS, each with level and
levelWithMax) and reads the opt-in via cfgProvidersAutoThinkingMaxEffort.
autoEffortCeiling() became a module constant AUTO_EFFORT_CEILING =
Effort.High used by both the local-bucket and online branches; the online
branch always asks `questions.level` (never levelWithMax). The now-unused
getSupportedEfforts / cfgProvidersAutoThinkingMaxEffort imports are
dropped. Test edits re-targeted to the `{ request }` input shape; same
assertions as before.

Rebased 18.11.1 -> 18.12.6: upstream filters the online ladder question to
the target model's supported tiers (filterSupportedLevels) and short-circuits
without a judge when zero or one tier remains. The ladder filter now also
drops tiers above AUTO_EFFORT_CEILING, so the short-circuits stay correct
under the high cap (e.g. `["xhigh", "max"]` resolves nothing instead of
xhigh) and the judge is offered low/medium/high only; an `xhigh` answer is
now off-ladder and rejects. Tests: the xhigh-caps-at-high case becomes
"never offers xhigh" (xhigh rejects, high resolves) plus an xhigh-and-up
ladder case; upstream's new sparse-ladder prompt test and the solution-space
test answer `high` instead of `max`/`xhigh`.

